## b221bb53 Carry the public link from the keeper, with the network's client id, and end its TLS there

The tunnel container goes. The keeper reads the link and its certificate from the database every 3 s, runs frpc as its
child under the client id the relay expects, and ends the link's TLS on 127.0.0.1:8443, so the app no longer holds a
listener or reads linkVisitor. A failed read keeps the frpc and the certificate the keeper has, and keeper.json names a
certificate only after the listener took it, so the app moves the link to https on what is really served.

Tests: the plan's Done when (client, tunnel, link-listener, keeper-link, link-tls, link, auth) passes, and the types print
nothing. Each new guard was seen red by breaking the code it guards.

Ruling: frpc gets only PATH and the client's own env, not the keeper's environment - why: frpc 0.71.1 fills in {{ .Envs.NAME }} in its config (checked with frpc verify), the config carries the key text the relay gave, and the keeper holds HORNHOOF_SECRET and DATABASE_URL, so the plan's process.env would let a relay's answer read them - cost if wrong: an frpc that needs another variable does not start, which shows at the first rehearsal.
Ruling: LinkListener.listen emits 'listening' on the server it hands connections to - why: Node times a connection's headers and finds its idle ones only on a server it saw listen, 1b's Fastify server listened and this third door does not, so a TLS visitor who never ends its headers would hold a connection for good - cost if wrong: it rests on Node's wiring, and the test on the 408 goes red on a Node that changes it.
Ruling: servedCertificate gives no certificate with ACME_DIRECTORY off, which the plan does not say - why: LinkTls.serveOn then asks use(host, null), and a stored certificate in the keeper's word would time KeeperServed out with a false warning - cost if wrong: with certificates off the listener serves a stand-in, which nobody reaches since frpc runs http then.
Ruling: a failed read or a certificate the listener refuses says one fixed line per outage, which the plan does not ask for - why: otherwise a keeper that cannot read its link says nothing at all, and a query's error can quote its values, so no detail goes out - cost if wrong: two more log lines in an outage.
Ruling: isTunnelAddress and the listener's socketAddress leave listener.ts, as isAddressOf in auth/tunnel.ts shared with the keeper, and net/proxy's socketAddress - why: the listener is now the keeper's alone, and the keeper had the same lookup a second time - cost if wrong: none in behaviour, the auth and keeper tests cover both callers.
Ruling: auth.test.ts is not changed - why: it has no cases on the listener's sockets, 1b's were in link-listener.test.ts and the path is now covered by keeper-link.test.ts - cost if wrong: none.
Ruling: keeper.test.ts is changed though the task does not list it - why: its preload patched only http.Server's listen, so the entry point would have bound the real 8443, it counted two doors, and it parsed every line of stdout as JSON while the keeper now logs there - cost if wrong: none.
Ruling: Settings loses the default of mode and tunnelSettings and writeTunnelSettings both parse through it - why: nothing reads an old tunnel.json any more, and data is checked where it enters - cost if wrong: a stored tunnel that is not a record fails the write with a log line instead of being written.

